  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 238 PM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nRain chances increase from west to east Friday and into the weekend \ncourtesy of an upper level system approaching the area out of the \nDesert Southwest. Increased lift and moisture with the trough brings \nlow (10-30%) chances for isolated showers and storms over our \nwestern counties Friday afternoon. Rain chances increase and spread\neastward Friday evening into Saturday, with the best odds \nfavoring the western half of our area. Models suggest the trough \nmeanders near our region through early next week, eventually \nlifting into the central and northern Plains sometime mid week. \nSeveral disturbances passing within the trough will provide \nsufficient lift to result in rounds of isolated to scattered \nshowers/storms almost areawide through Tuesday. With ridging \nremaining to our east, convection may struggle to maintain itself \nover the eastern portions of our area. Given a potential for \nrounds of occasionally heavy rainfall, localized flash flooding \nwill be of concern for SE NM through the weekend, especially on \nSaturday. Otherwise, temperatures look to gradually cool through \nthe course of the Long Term period due to lowering heights and an \neventual cold front. As of now, highs largely in the 80s Friday \nare forecast to give way to highs in the 60s and 70s by Tuesday.",
